    "question": {
      ":question/id": "66b00a87-f753-525d-b4af-dd1ba89c36e4",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=- \\frac{19}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-4.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(-4)=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{11}{2}-(4)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=-4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{3}{2})-(-4)=- \\frac{19}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=- \\frac{19}{2}-(-3)-(4)=- \\frac{21}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=- \\frac{21}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=-4.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{21}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
