    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2d3a11f6-f45f-5997-b0dd-4bba8bc1ec30",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(3)=-3.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-3-(3)=-6.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=-6$ and ${x}_{3}=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(-6)-(3)=5.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=5-(6)-(-3)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=-6$, and ${x}_{3}=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨2,-6,3⟩.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
